{
  "requirement": "Outlining reads one file snapshot and parses its supplied bytes. Text search\nlists files in scope and searches their bytes. Structural search lists files\nin scope and parses their supplied snapshots. These operations share the\nfilesystem scope under {cite}`requirements.sdk.sources.local_provider`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement has four obligations. Each maps to a test with assertions that enforce it.\n\n1. **Outlining reads one snapshot and parses its supplied bytes.** In `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/tests.rs#fn[defaults_use_provider_primitives_and_keep_one_parse_snapshot]`, `Memory.parse` performs exactly one read (`reads == 1`). The parsed source and outline keep \"version 0\" after a later read returns \"version 1\". `#fn[list_read_only_provider_supports_selected_language_parse_and_structural_search]` checks that parsing a list/read-only `Primitives` provider uses the bytes it supplies.\n\n2. **Text search lists files in scope and searches their bytes.** The same `Memory` test searches with `SourceScope::under(\"src\")`. The event log is `list:src`, then a read of `src/one.txt` only. `Memory.list` also returns `other/two.txt`, which is never read, so out-of-scope files are not searched. The assertions check two matches, the path, the line and the excerpt, which come from the bytes that were read.\n\n3. **Structural search lists files in scope and parses their supplied snapshots.** `#fn[structural_search_uses_overridden_parses_their_snapshot_and_requested_scope]` uses `CachedParse`. Its `read` returns an error, so any refetch would fail the test. `parses == 3` for three queries over a listing of two files shows that only the in-scope file is parsed, once per query. The scope seen by `list` is asserted as \"src\". Match path, selector and excerpt are asserted, and limit and completeness are checked. The `Primitives` test also runs structural search over a list/read-only provider. It covers a mismatched resource error, a malformed rule, and language mismatch and unknown-language cases.\n\n4. **The operations share the filesystem scope under `requirements.sdk.sources.local_provider`.** This is a cited requirement, so I checked only that the operations are tested under that scope. `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/source_defaults.rs#fn[gitignore_is_shared_by_all_operations_with_or_without_a_git_repository]` runs list, read, parse and text search against the real `Sources` provider. It covers nested ignores and their exceptions, ignored files that Git tracks, `.git` and `..`, direct file and subdirectory scopes, and runs with and without a Git repository. `#fn[built_in_languages_and_structural_search_use_the_project_scope]` covers structural search under the same scope: ignored files, `skip`, `.git`, `..`, a direct file scope, and results for multiple files.\n\nI found no unmarked relevant tests and no untested obligation.",
  "references": "052089c3443473951aef64cc577887d2fb947f5c6b07da6b25d20566dfc43caf",
